<ContentPage
  title="About — CodeXX DTDK"
  description="About William Karol Di Cioccio — a 19-year-old self-taught Italian programmer building CodeXX DTDK, with a heart that has always belonged to C++."
  eyebrow="About Us (me, for now)"
  heading="Hi — I'm William."
  lead="William Karol Di Cioccio. Nineteen, twenty soon. Italian, self-taught, and building CodeXX DTDK largely on my own — for now."
  active="about"
>
  <p>
    I started programming when I was <strong>13</strong>, and I've been
    self-taught the whole way. These days I work as a <strong>Flutter developer
    and UX/UI designer</strong> for an American company — good work — but it
    isn't where my heart is.
  </p>

  <p>
    My heart has always been with <strong>C++</strong>. I grew up watching
    <strong>The Cherno</strong>, and I'm not ashamed to say I'm emotionally
    attached to the language. I find the core language genuinely
    <strong>elegant and easy to reason about</strong>; and its low-level control
    and deep integration with the platforms it runs on — all the legacy it
    carries included — make me feel <strong>powerful, free to build whatever I
    want</strong>. Maybe it's simply that C++ was the first language I ever truly
    picked up. Either way, CodeXX is a love letter to it.
  </p>

  <p class="pull">
    CodeXX is the project my day job doesn't leave room for — so I make the room.
  </p>

  <p>
    Doing this solo means being deliberate about where my time goes: test-driven
    from the start, tooling that dogfoods itself, and modern tools used
    aggressively so a one-person effort can move at the scale the idea deserves.
    (More on exactly how, and where the lines are drawn, on the
    <a href="/ai">On AI</a> page.)
  </p>

  <p>
    "For now" is doing a lot of work in that heading. The ambition is bigger than
    one person — but everything you see here started with one, and I'd rather
    build it honestly and in the open than pretend otherwise.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
